ain, but since it's already at EOF from the first read, it would correctly return nothing. That's the expected behavior.\n\nNow I'm checking the imports—I need `bufio`, `flag`, `fmt`, `io`, `os`, `os/signal`, and `syscall` for handling `SIGPIPE`, all from the standard library.\n\nLooking at the project's go.mod, there are no external dependencies, so I should stick with the standard library. `syscall.SIGPIPE` is available on POSIX systems, so that works fine. Time to write the implementation.\n\nI need to create the `cmd/cat/` directory structure with `cmd/cat/main.go`. The package should be `package main` since it's a command binary. Let me verify the flag variable won't cause linting issues—actually, I should check if unused flag variables are flagged by golangci-lint.\n\nActually, I realize that `flag.BoolVar(&flagUnbuffered, ...)` takes the address of the variable, which counts as using it, so the compiler won't complain. Most linters should accept this pattern too. the variable is being used through its address. Since `flag.BoolVar` takes a pointer to `flagUnbuffered`, the compiler considers it \"used\" even though we never explicitly read its value afterward, so there's no unused variable issue.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the error handling approach. I'm noticing that my code calls `fmt.Fprintf(os.Stderr, ...)` in several places without checking errors, but looking at the existing codebase patterns, some files skip error checks for stderr writes while others explicitly ignore them with blank assignments. Since writing to stderr is best-effort anyway, I need to decide which pattern to follow here. I'm settling on matching the style from cmd/version/main.go and skipping the `_, _ =` pattern for stderr writes since errcheck might not be enabled for those. Now I'll write out the final implementation using the standard flag.CommandLine since it's simpler and sufficient for this use case.\n\nWriting the implementation... Defining the configuration struct with flags